{{Top}}
[[File:Concord_Dawn_thumb.jpg|thumb|right|300px|[[Sabine Wren]] in [[Sabine Wren's armor|her armor]] slides a rangefinder over [[Sabine Wren's modified Nite Owl helmet|her helmet's]] viewplate.]]
'''Macrobinocular viewplates''',<ref name="RVG">''[[Star Wars Rebels: The Visual Guide]]''</ref> also known as a '''macrobinocular visor''',<ref name="Master of Evil">''[[Master of Evil]]''</ref> a '''T-shaped visor''',<ref name="TROS VD">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref> or as '''T-visors''',<ref name="Inside Intel">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-inside-intel-mandalorian-armor|text=''Star Wars'' Inside Intel: Mandalorian Armor|int=Star Wars Inside Intel}}</ref> were visors that could be found on [[Mandalorian]] [[Mandalorian armor|helmets]], shaped like the letter [[High Galactic|T]]. A [[rangefinder]] could be pulled over the viewplate to enhance imagery.<ref name="USW">''[[Ultimate Star Wars]]''</ref> They could also be found on [[clone trooper armor]] and [[Sith trooper armor]].<ref name="TROS VD" /> The [[Inquisitorius|Imperial Inquisitor]], known as the [[Fourth Sister]] had a [[helmet]] that had a red T-shaped visor. This helmet was worn by [[Lina Graf]].<ref name="Return to Vader's Castle 42">[[Star Wars Adventures: Return to Vader's Castle 4|''Star Wars Adventures: Return to Vader's Castle'' 4]]</ref>
{{Tech-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
Macrobinocular viewplates made their debut in the [[1980]] [[original trilogy]] film, [[Star Wars: Episode V The Empire Strikes Back|''Star Wars'': Episode V ''The Empire Strikes Back'']].<ref name="ESB">{{Film|V}}</ref> They were first [[canon]]ically identified in the [[2014]] reference book ''[[Star Wars Rebels: The Visual Guide]]'', written by [[Adam Bray]].<ref name="RVG" />

